<section class="legal legal-wide"><span class="eyebrow">Privacy</span><h1>Privacy Policy</h1><p class="lead">This Privacy Policy explains how PocketCat may collect, use, store and share information when you use the PocketCat website, workspace, administrative console and optional AI features. PocketCat is an early-stage service based in India, so this document is written to describe the product as it is intended to operate today and may evolve as the service matures.</p>
<h2>1. Scope</h2><p>This policy applies to information processed through PocketCat public pages, account creation, authentication, tasks, notes, projects, contact forms, administrative features, analytics and AI assistant requests. It also applies to information that an administrator enters into service configuration fields, such as an Amazon Bedrock API key, AWS credentials, SMTP credentials or an endpoint URL. It does not give you permission to submit information that you are not authorized to disclose.</p>
<h2>2. Information you provide</h2><p>When you create an account, PocketCat may process your name, email address, username, password hash, timezone, account status and subscription plan. Passwords are stored as password hashes rather than readable passwords. When you use workspace features, PocketCat stores the tasks, notes, projects, milestones and related records that you choose to create. When you contact PocketCat, the service may store your name, email address, subject, message, IP address and submission time.</p>
<p>Administrators may configure optional integrations. These can include an Amazon Bedrock API key, AWS access key, secret key, temporary session token, region, model identifier and compatible custom endpoint. Email settings can include SMTP host, port, username, password and sender information. Sensitive integration values are intended to be stored server-side and encrypted at rest by the application. Administrators should still use credentials with the minimum practical permissions and rotate credentials according to their own security requirements.</p>
<h2>3. Automatically generated information</h2><p>PocketCat may create operational records when users sign in, create tasks, create notes, create projects, use the AI assistant or interact with administrative features. These records can include event type, account identifier, IP address and timestamp. Such information is useful for troubleshooting, security review, product analytics and understanding whether core features are functioning. The public landing page may also display aggregate counters such as registered accounts, active accounts and activity totals. Those counters are generated from application data and may refresh periodically while the page is open.</p>
<h2>4. How information is used</h2><p>Information may be used to authenticate accounts, provide the requested workspace, save and retrieve user content, enforce account status, maintain security controls, operate administrator functions, troubleshoot failures, measure product activity, provide customer support and improve the service. AI prompts are processed only when an AI provider is configured and a user invokes an AI feature. Email information is used when an email service is configured and a product workflow needs to send mail.</p>
<h2>5. AI and Amazon Bedrock</h2><p>PocketCat treats AI as an optional service. When Amazon Bedrock is configured, prompts and the relevant request payload may be transmitted to the configured Bedrock runtime or compatible endpoint. The response is then returned to the PocketCat user interface. Depending on the configured provider, model and account arrangement, that provider may process the request under its own terms and privacy documentation. Users should avoid submitting secrets, passwords, financial information, confidential customer data or other sensitive material unless they have authority to do so and understand the provider arrangement.</p>
<p>PocketCat may estimate AI usage from input and output text for subscription enforcement. The free plan is designed around a daily allowance of approximately 2 hours of active AI usage per day, calculated using an application-side character-based estimate rather than a provider-specific tokenizer. Premium accounts can receive a higher or effectively unlimited allowance subject to the commercial plan and infrastructure. The usage record is tied to the account and date so the application can enforce the selected plan.</p>
<h2>6. Credentials and security</h2><p>Integration secrets should never be placed into frontend JavaScript, public source files or client-visible HTML. PocketCat is designed to keep configured AI and SMTP secrets on the server. Sensitive settings are encrypted using an application secret stored outside normal public pages. Even with these safeguards, no internet service can guarantee absolute security. Account owners remain responsible for selecting strong passwords, protecting credentials, limiting administrator access and promptly rotating compromised keys.</p>
<h2>7. Sharing and service providers</h2><p>PocketCat may share information with service providers only where needed to operate a feature you request or to host and secure the application. For example, an AI prompt may be sent to the configured Amazon Bedrock endpoint, and an email may be handed to the configured SMTP service. Hosting infrastructure may necessarily process application data while serving requests and storing the database. PocketCat does not describe these providers as part of a blanket promise that all data remains on a single server or in a single country.</p>
<h2>8. Cookies and sessions</h2><p>PocketCat uses session cookies for authentication and security. Necessary cookies help the service remember that you are signed in and help protect forms against cross-site request forgery. The public site may use only minimal functionality unless additional analytics or preference mechanisms are introduced. Browser settings can be used to restrict cookies, although disabling necessary session cookies can prevent account features from working.</p>
<h2>9. Retention</h2><p>Information may be retained for as long as an account or workspace remains active and for a reasonable period needed for backups, security, dispute handling and operational records. Retention periods can vary by information type. Contact messages may be retained so that support requests can be answered. Activity logs may be retained to investigate abuse or technical problems. When a workspace is deleted, associated records may be removed according to application behavior and database relationships, while backups or security records may persist for a limited operational period.</p>
<h2>10. Your choices and responsibilities</h2><p>You can update profile information through account settings where the feature is available. You can choose whether to use optional AI functionality. You should review generated AI responses before relying on them. You are responsible for ensuring that workspace content and prompts do not violate law, confidentiality obligations or the rights of other people. If you manage a team, you are responsible for deciding which users should have administrator privileges.</p>
<h2>11. Children and sensitive information</h2><p>PocketCat is a general productivity service and is not designed to collect sensitive information from children. Do not use the service to intentionally collect or expose another person's sensitive information without an appropriate legal basis or authorization. If you believe personal information has been submitted improperly, contact the support address so the situation can be reviewed.</p>
<h2>12. International processing</h2><p>PocketCat is based in India, but hosting and configured third-party services can involve infrastructure outside India. The exact location of processing can depend on the hosting provider, AWS region, SMTP provider and any custom endpoint selected by an administrator. Users should select service regions and providers consistent with their organization\'s requirements.</p>
<h2>13. Indian privacy framework</h2><p>This policy is written with the Indian privacy framework in mind, including the Digital Personal Data Protection framework and related principles. It is not a legal certification, a statement that every statutory obligation has been satisfied, or legal advice. Organizations using PocketCat should obtain their own professional advice where a specific compliance determination is required.</p>
<h2>14. Data incidents</h2><p>If PocketCat becomes aware of a security incident affecting the service, the response may include investigating the scope, limiting access, rotating credentials, restoring service and communicating with affected parties where appropriate. Administrators should immediately rotate third-party keys if they suspect exposure and should avoid posting credentials in support requests.</p>
<h2>15. Changes to this policy</h2><p>As PocketCat adds features, changes infrastructure or introduces new providers, this policy may be revised. The current version published on the website is the version intended to describe the service at that time. Continued use after a material update may be treated as acceptance where applicable, subject to mandatory rights under law.</p>
<h2>16. Contact</h2><p>For privacy questions, requests or concerns, contact <strong>support@pocketcat.online</strong>. Please do not send passwords, API keys or secret credentials in an email or contact form.</p></section>
